Requirements
Project: STEP – Segmented Teaching for Every Person Course: Applications Development and Emerging Technologies Status: Draft for Sprint 1

1. Overview
STEP is a web-based learning platform, designed to work alongside an existing LMS, that turns long lecture videos and text-heavy PDFs into short, interactive, accessible micro-lessons. It is designed for college students who struggle to stay focused, including students with ADHD, and every feature is available to every student without a diagnosis or accommodation request.

Problem
Traditional LMS courses rely on static, text-heavy PDFs and long lecture videos. This causes lost focus, digital fatigue, and poor retention. The interactive tools that could help often lack basic accessibility such as screen reader support and keyboard navigation, or add time pressure and distraction, so students with disabilities and learning differences cannot take part fully.

Users
User	Needs
Student (primary)	Short lessons, one clear next step, immediate calm feedback, a choice of formats, accessibility from the start, no disclosure required
Instructor	A simple way to turn existing videos and PDFs into short lessons, write check-in questions, and see where students drop off
Core use cases
An instructor uploads a video or PDF, reviews the suggested segments, adds check-in questions, and publishes the lesson.
A student enrolls in a course, studies a lesson one segment at a time, answers a quick question after each, and resumes later exactly where they stopped.
A student switches between watching, listening, and reading, and saves display preferences that apply to every course.
A student sees quiet progress (streaks, effort points, badges) without timers or public competition.
