3. Feature Details
E1 Accounts & Enrollment
ACC-01 Account registration and login (Must)
User story: As a student or instructor, I want to create an account and log in, so that my courses and progress belong to me.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] A user can register with a name, email, and password.
[ ] Passwords must be at least 8 characters and are never stored in plain text.
[ ] A duplicate email is rejected, and emails are not case-sensitive.
[ ] A wrong email and a wrong password show the same error message.
[ ] Sign-up never asks about a diagnosis or disability.
Dependencies: none

ACC-02 Course enrollment (Must)
User story: As a student, I want to enroll in a course, so that I can study its lessons.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] A student can enroll in a course once, and a duplicate enrollment is rejected.
[ ] A student can drop a course they are enrolled in.
[ ] Enrolled courses appear on the student's dashboard.
[ ] An instructor can see the roster of a course.
Dependencies: ACC-01

E2 Content & Lessons
CNT-01 Semi-automatic content chunking (Must)
User story: As an instructor, I want the system to suggest short segments from my video or PDF, so that I can turn long content into bite-sized lessons without splitting it by hand.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] An instructor can upload a video lesson (with a transcript or caption file) or a PDF lesson (with its extracted text).
[ ] The system suggests segments of about 250 words, using the transcript for videos and the text for PDFs.
[ ] Breaks fall only at sentence ends, and a manual break that would cut a sentence is rejected.
[ ] The instructor can move break points and must confirm the segments before publishing.
[ ] Each segment shows its estimated length (real duration for video, reading time for text).
[ ] Nothing is saved until the instructor confirms.
Dependencies: ACC-01

CNT-02 PDF study tools (Should)
User story: As a student studying a PDF lesson, I want flashcards and expandable details, so that I can review key ideas without rereading everything.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] An instructor can add flashcards (front and back) to a PDF card.
[ ] Flashcards flip using the mouse and the keyboard.
[ ] "Read more" sections open and close with the keyboard, and screen readers announce their state.
Dependencies: CNT-01

CNT-03 Visual summaries (Could)
User story: As a student who learns visually, I want a diagram or infographic of the lesson, so that I can understand it without dense text.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] An instructor can attach an image (diagram, mind map, or infographic) to a lesson.
[ ] Alt text is required before the image can be saved.
[ ] The visual summary appears as a third format option for the student.
Dependencies: CNT-01, ACS-03

E3 Interactive Learning
INT-01 Check-in questions with immediate feedback (Must)
User story: As a student, I want a quick question at the end of each segment with instant feedback, so that I can check my understanding and remember more.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Each segment has at most one question with 2 to 4 answer options, written by the instructor.
[ ] A video lesson cannot be published unless every segment has a question.
[ ] Feedback appears immediately, uses calm wording, and shows the correct answer and an explanation.
[ ] There is no timer, score, or penalty, and the student can retry.
[ ] Only an enrolled student can answer, and only on a published lesson.
[ ] The correct answer is not sent to the student before they answer.
[ ] The question can be answered by keyboard and is announced by screen readers.
Dependencies: CNT-01, ACC-02

INT-02 Resume where you left off (Must)
User story: As a student with limited time, I want to resume exactly where I stopped, so that I never lose my place.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Leaving a lesson saves the student's position.
[ ] Reopening the lesson, even after logging out and back in, returns to the same place.
[ ] The dashboard shows a single "Continue" action for the next step.
Dependencies: PRG-01

INT-03 Spaced review questions (Could)
User story: As a student, I want old questions to come back over time, so that I remember what I learned.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Questions from earlier lessons reappear after set intervals.
[ ] A student can skip a review without any penalty or lost streak.
[ ] Review results are private.
Dependencies: INT-01, PRG-01

E4 Accessibility & Multimodal Access
ACS-01 Captions and searchable transcripts (Must)
User story: As a student who is deaf, hard of hearing, or sensitive to sound, I want captions and a searchable transcript, so that I can follow a video without relying on audio.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Captions show by default, using the caption file supplied with the lesson, and can be turned off.
[ ] A transcript panel is shown next to the video.
[ ] A search box filters the transcript.
[ ] Clicking a transcript line jumps the video to that time.
Dependencies: CNT-01

ACS-02 WCAG 2.1 AA-aligned interface (Must)
User story: As a student who uses a keyboard or screen reader, I want every feature to work for me from the start, so that I can take part fully.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Every function works with the keyboard alone, with a visible focus indicator and no keyboard traps.
[ ] Controls, images, and form fields have accessible names and labels.
[ ] Text contrast is at least 4.5:1 (3:1 for large text and UI components), and meaning never depends on color alone.
[ ] Text can be enlarged to 200% without losing content or function.
[ ] Nothing flashes.
[ ] Verified with Lighthouse, axe, and a keyboard-only walkthrough, with the results recorded.
Dependencies: none (applies to all interface work)

ACS-03 Multimodal alternatives (Should)
User story: As a student who finds audio or dense text hard to process, I want to switch between watching, listening, and reading, so that I can learn in the way that suits me.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] A student can switch between formats in one click, at any point in a lesson.
[ ] Text-to-speech reads the instructor-written text version, with play, pause, and speed controls.
[ ] Video playback speed is adjustable.
[ ] Audio is never required to finish a lesson.
Dependencies: CNT-01, ACS-02

ACS-04 Low-stimulation mode (Should)
User story: As a student who is easily overwhelmed, I want to turn off animations and sounds, so that I can concentrate.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] One toggle disables all animations and interface sound effects.
[ ] The setting respects the operating system's "reduce motion" preference.
[ ] The setting is saved.
Dependencies: ACS-05

ACS-05 Saved universal preferences (Should)
User story: As a student, I want my display and format settings remembered in every course, so that I never have to ask for an accommodation.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] A student can set font size, spacing, contrast, and preferred lesson format.
[ ] Preferences apply to all of the student's courses and take effect immediately.
[ ] Preferences are private and need no approval or disclosure.
Dependencies: ACC-01

E5 Progress & Motivation
PRG-01 Automatic progress tracking and personal dashboard (Must)
User story: As a student, I want to see how far I have come and what to do next, so that I stay motivated.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Finishing a segment and answering its question is recorded automatically.
[ ] The dashboard shows each enrolled course with its percent complete.
[ ] The dashboard shows one clear next step.
[ ] Progress is kept between sessions.
Dependencies: ACC-02, INT-01

PRG-02 Calm gamification (Should)
User story: As a student, I want quiet rewards for consistency, so that I stay motivated without pressure.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] A streak counts consecutive days with activity.
[ ] Effort points reward completing segments, not speed.
[ ] Simple badges mark consistency milestones.
[ ] There are no countdown timers, leaderboards, or flashing effects.
[ ] A broken streak shows a neutral, welcoming message.
Dependencies: PRG-01, ACS-04

PRG-03 In-app reminders (Could)
User story: As a student who forgets to return, I want a gentle reminder about unfinished lessons, so that I keep going.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] A banner lists unfinished lessons.
[ ] The student controls how often reminders appear and can turn them off.
[ ] Reminders never use countdowns or urgent wording.
Dependencies: PRG-01

E6 Instructor Insights
INS-01 Instructor drop-off dashboard (Could)
User story: As an instructor, I want to see where students stop, so that I can shorten or fix confusing content.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] The dashboard shows how many students completed each segment of a lesson.
[ ] Individual students are not identified.
[ ] The segment with the largest drop is highlighted.
Dependencies: PRG-01

4. MVP Scope
The first working release includes every Must feature, which together let an instructor publish a chunked lesson and a student complete it:

Order	Feature	Why it is essential
1	ACC-01 Accounts	Everything else needs a signed-in user
2	ACC-02 Enrollment	Connects students to courses
3	CNT-01 Chunking	The core idea of the product
4	INT-01 Check-in questions	Turns viewing into active recall
5	ACS-01 Captions and transcripts	Accessibility for video
6	ACS-02 WCAG-aligned interface	Accessibility is not an add-on
7	PRG-01 Progress tracking	Powers the dashboard
8	INT-02 Resume	Depends on progress tracking
Should features follow once the MVP works end to end. Could features are stretch goals.

5. Out of Scope for This Release (Won't)
A true LMS plug-in (for example Moodle or Canvas). A possible future path is LTI integration.
Automatically generated captions, transcripts, summaries, or flashcards. Instructors supply them.
Push or email notifications. Reminders are in-app only.
Leaderboards, countdown timers, and any public competition.
Any question about a student's diagnosis or disability.
6. Assumptions and Constraints
STEP is a standalone web application that works alongside an LMS, not inside one.
Video lessons need a transcript or caption file from the instructor. Chunking depends on it.
The 250-word target is a default that an instructor can change.
"WCAG-aligned" means the interface is designed and tested against WCAG 2.1 Level AA, not formally certified.
Sign-in and storage are course-project grade. A production version would use a dedicated authentication provider and a real database.
